9 stories: ‘Preserve this year’s stocks’: 1.25 million Zambians face hunger in the lean season as the Government activates its El Niño plan — but the Vice-President says the lights stay on (lead); ‘We do not break. We come together’: Hichilema’s message for Zambia at 62 — and the opposition’s answer that there is nothing yet to celebrate; Three security zones for one constituency: the police lay out their plan for the Mandevu by-election on 5 November after the Tonse Alliance says its candidate was attacked; ‘How do you ask “how did they apprehend?”’: the Vice-President tells MPs the Government will not explain Jay Jay Banda’s capture — and says the lean Cabinet is working; US$122 million for Kalulushi: the ZDA signs a protection agreement with Rongxing to double a cathode plant, as the Mines PS says existing mines cannot deliver three million tonnes; Out of Chimbokaila and back to court: Nakacinda’s ECZ summons trial is put off to 25 November after his lawyer says he is too ill, and the magistrate calls it the last adjournment; History from twelve yards: Red Arrows lose 1–0 in Durban, level the tie and beat Kaizer Chiefs 3–2 on penalties to reach a CAF group stage for the first time; Three games, three defeats, no points: Lea Motyka’s early goal gives France a 1–0 win and ends the Copper Princesses’ U-17 Women’s World Cup; Mpasela sinks the champions: MUZA beat Power Dynamos 1–0 to lead Stream A, Trident score six and ZESCO finally win in week five of the transitional Super League.

